  "text": "umably, we can find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ by equating the numerator and denominator of the left-hand side to those of the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, we obtain the polynomial $q(x)$ equal to the numerator of the right-hand side:\n\n$$q(x) = x^3+320x^2-7380x-10080 $$\nTo check for the roots of $q(x)$, we can use the rational root theorem. By trial and error, we find that \n$$x_1 = 120$$\n$$x_2 = -27$$\n$$x_3 = 8$$\nwhere the earlier solution for $x=x_1=-27$ was incorrect.\n\nNow, we obtain the factor for $q(x)$ by recognizing these roots and their multiplicities which are equivalent to the factors for the denominators:\n\n$$\\frac{q(x)}{x^3-120x^2+2718x-32400} = \\left ( \\frac{x-120}{x^3-120x^2+2718x-32400} \\cdot \\left ( \\frac{q(x)}{x^3-120x^2+2718x-32400} \\right ) \\right )$$\n$$= \\left ( \\frac{x-120}{x^3-120x^2+2718x-32400} \\cdot \\left ( \\frac{x^3+320x^2-7380x-10080}{x^3-120x^2+2718x-32400} \\right ) \\right )$$\n$$=1$$\n\nThis equality implies that the left-hand side of the identity can be expressed as a fraction with a denominator of $(x-120)(x-120)(x-120)$. Now, we need to find $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ from the terms of the constant middle term and the constant term from the following polynomial:\n\n$$\\frac{x+\\alpha}{(x-120)(x-120)(x-\\beta)}$$\nThis polynomial has a constant middle term of $\\frac{\\alpha x + \\alpha_x}{x^2} + \\frac{\\beta_x}{x^2}$ and a constant term of $\\frac{\\alpha x + \\alpha_x}{x} + \\frac{\\beta}{x}$. The first three parts of these expressions were found at previous steps. Secondly, we can set $\\frac{$\\alpha_x}{x} =$\n.SetFloatVariable('{}', '\\alpha');\n\n$\n\n.SetFloatVariable('{}', '\\beta');\n\n$",
